### Part 3: Topics Missed Due to Interruption (For Your Follow-Up Paper)

Because of the fire alarm, you missed the final ~5 minutes of the planned runbook. Your follow-up paper should cover these three specific items to "close the loop."

**1. Closing the Loop (Syncing back to VS Code)**
*   *What was missed:* You merged the Pull Request on the GitHub website, updating `main` in the cloud. However, you didn't have time to go back to VS Code, switch your local branch back to `main`, and click "Sync" to pull that merged change back down to your laptop. 
*   *Why it matters:* If they don't do this, their laptop becomes out of sync with production. This is the #1 cause of Git errors for beginners.

**2. The Audit Trail (The History Tab)**
*   *What was missed:* Showing the "History" (clock icon) button on GitHub.
*   *Why it matters:* This proves the "Code is the Documentation" concept. You need to show them that GitHub keeps a permanent, timestamped log of every commit message.

**3. The "Undo" Button (Answering Jim's Question)**
*   *What was missed:* Explaining the "Revert" function.
*   *Why it matters:* Jim wants to know how to fix a mistake. You can explain that in GitHub, you can look at the History, find the bad commit, and click **Revert**. GitHub will automatically generate a *new* commit that mathematically undoes whatever the bad commit did, safely rolling the network back to the previous state.